    unit_model: {
      recursive: "the design layer is a recursively self-similar TREE of units. A unit is the same shape at every altitude \u2014 an overview plus its child units plus a pointer to its parent. 'context', 'component', and an NN.M subsection are depth labels on ONE node type, not different things. The root indexes contexts; a context indexes its components; a component indexes its NN.M sub-parts; the recursion continues until a unit passes the reproduction test (design_doc.completeness).",
      index_children_only: "a unit's overview indexes ONLY its own children. A reference to a unit that is not its child is always an explicit POINTER \u2014 down to a child, up to an ancestor, or lateral to another unit \u2014 never a silent inclusion and never a silent omission. A spanning index (a 'whole set at a glance') that lists some off-node units and drops others is an authoring bug: either point to every off-node unit or scope the section to this unit's own children, and state which. This is the general rule; a domain document half-listing sibling-context-owned units is one instance of violating it.",
      lateral_graph: "OVERLAID on the recursive tree is a lateral REFERENCE GRAPH \u2014 CONTEXT terms owned by one unit and cited by others, seams between sibling units, and the DDD relationships (the set is owned by layer_layout.multi_context.context_map) recorded in the context map. These lateral edges are NOT the recursion (the tree gives containment/disclosure; the graph gives cross-references) and are not the file split either.",
      three_concerns_distinct: "keep three concerns separate, never conflated: (1) RECURSION governs containment and progressive disclosure (this model); (2) the FILE SPLIT \u2014 where the recursion breaks into separate design.md/CONTEXT.md files \u2014 is domain_discovery's second-real-vocabulary rule, a semantic judgment, not a depth counter; (3) the LATERAL GRAPH is cross-reference. A unit can recurse deeply inside one file (NN.M.K) without a file split, and can hold lateral edges without those being parent/child."
