    completeness: {
      two_axes: "completeness has two distinct axes, both required. BREADTH (coverage): every meaningful system or subsystem appears in the design layer \u2014 captured, or explicitly annotated as intentionally-not-detailed. DEPTH (reproduction grade): each captured unit is deep enough to rebuild. Depth without breadth is a layer that describes some parts perfectly and is blind to others; breadth without depth is an index with no design. A completeness pass checks BOTH.",
      breadth: {
        property: "no meaningful part of the system is invisible to the design layer. The question breadth asks: is there any subsystem the design is blind to? Every part is accounted for in the coverage map (layer_layout.coverage_map) with one status \u2014 captured, standard, or out-of-scope. An unaccounted part is uncovered drift (drift_kind), never a silent omission.",
        capture_everything_default: "the default is to capture everything. The deciding question for any part is the reproduction test: would an independent agent, handed the design layer alone, need this part's DESIGN to rebuild an equivalent system? A part carries real logic or non-obvious design (its shape was a decision) -> capture it. A part is plain, conventional infrastructure whose shape a competent builder would reproduce the same way from its mere presence (standard dependency manifests and lockfiles, standard build/test/CI wiring, formatter/linter config, ignore and license files, vendored third-party) -> a candidate for standard. This is GENERIC over any stack \u2014 the categories are 'carries logic / design decisions' vs 'vanilla infrastructure', never a fixed tool list; a bespoke or complex build pipeline, a non-trivial dependency-fetching scheme, or custom CI logic is itself design and should be captured. A part is marked standard or out-of-scope ONLY by an explicit decision: the agent proposes the status with its reason ('this looks standard \u2014 skip it from the design?'), and the user rules \u2014 tracking is always available if the user wants it, and the agent recommends capturing anything that looks less-than-standard. Every mark is explicit and revisitable \u2014 a standard part that grows non-standard is promoted to captured. Being explicit, not implicit, is the point.",
        boilerplate_note: "'internals derivable from a captured unit (algorithm bodies, boilerplate within it)' are legitimately absent from DEPTH \u2014 a depth exclusion inside a covered unit, not a breadth decision. A whole standard subsystem left undetailed is a BREADTH decision recorded in the coverage map."
      },
      test: "the reproduction test (the DEPTH axis): a third party holding only the layer \u2014 design document, CONTEXT, ADRs, no source \u2014 converges on an equivalent implementation of every CAPTURED part: the same components, interfaces, interactions, held invariants, and failure behavior. Internals derivable from those (algorithm bodies, boilerplate) are legitimately absent; abstractions, contracts, state, and interactions never are",
      unit_shape: "each design unit's subsection answers: what it is responsible for; the interface it exposes; who it interacts with and how; the invariants it holds; how it fails. A STATEFUL unit additionally models its state machine COMPLETELY \u2014 the states AND the transitions between them (edges, their guards/triggers, and re-entries) \u2014 never just the list of state names. A schema-declared state enum (run_mode, work_order_state, triage_roles) whose transitions live only in an implementation file is a depth gap: the edge set is load-bearing and underivable from the node list alone.",
      depth_stop: "a section is deep enough exactly when its unit passes the reproduction test \u2014 never a page budget, never a fixed level count",
      applied_by: "sync (onboard scopes coverage with the user first, then derives captured parts to reproduction grade; the probe audits BOTH axes), review-design (breadth pass over the coverage map + per-unit reproduction walk), new-design (the fixpoint includes the intended coverage), incremental updates (a meaningful new subsystem enters the coverage map before it is built)"
